<article class="article">
<header class="article-head">
<p class="kicker"><span>Condition</span><span aria-hidden="true">·</span><a href="/condition/#sys-injuries">Injuries &amp; poisoning</a></p>
<h1>Abdominal Trauma: Blunt and Penetrating Belly Injuries</h1>
<p class="lead"><strong>Abdominal trauma</strong> is any injury to the belly from a blow, fall, crash, stab or gunshot. The danger is damage you cannot see: bleeding from the spleen or liver, or a torn bowel that leaks into the abdomen. Signs can be delayed for hours. After a significant impact, belly pain, a tender or swelling abdomen, bruising from a seat belt, dizziness or blood in the urine need emergency assessment, even if you feel fine at first.</p>
<p class="also">Also called: Abdominal injury, belly injury, stomach injury, blunt abdominal trauma, penetrating abdominal trauma</p>
<p class="byline">By the <a href="/about/">iSymptom editorial team</a> · Updated <time datetime="2026-10-09">October 9, 2026</time> · 5 min read</p>
</header>
<div class="article-grid">
<aside class="article-side"><nav class="toc" aria-label="On this page"><details open><summary>On this page</summary><ol><li><a href="#first-aid-for-abdominal-injuries">First aid for abdominal injuries</a></li><li><a href="#types-of-abdominal-trauma">Types of abdominal trauma</a></li><li><a href="#organs-at-risk-and-their-signs">Organs at risk and their signs</a></li><li><a href="#symptoms-of-internal-injury">Symptoms of internal injury</a></li><li><a href="#how-abdominal-trauma-is-diagnosed">How abdominal trauma is diagnosed</a></li><li><a href="#treatment">Treatment</a></li><li><a href="#complications">Complications</a></li><li><a href="#prevention">Prevention</a></li><li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li></ol></details></nav></aside>
<div class="article-main">
<section class="glance" aria-label="At a glance"><h2 class="glance-title">At a glance</h2><dl><div><dt>What it is</dt><dd>Injury to the abdominal wall or organs from blunt force or a penetrating wound.</dd></div><div><dt>Common causes</dt><dd>Car crashes, falls, sports collisions, assaults, bicycle handlebars, stabbings.</dd></div><div><dt>Most often injured</dt><dd>Spleen and liver in blunt trauma; bowel and liver in stab wounds.</dd></div><div><dt>Danger signs</dt><dd>Worsening pain, a rigid or swollen belly, dizziness, fast pulse, blood in urine or vomit.</dd></div><div><dt>Diagnosis and treatment</dt><dd>FAST ultrasound and CT scans; observation, embolization or surgery.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section class="triage" aria-labelledby="triage-h"><h2 id="triage-h" class="triage-title">When to get help</h2><div class="triage-grid"><div class="tri tri--red"><h3>Call 911 or go to the ER</h3><ul><li>Any stab or gunshot wound to the belly, back, flank or lower chest: call 911 or your local emergency number</li><li>Belly pain after a crash, fall from height, or blow, with dizziness, fainting, pale clammy skin or a fast pulse (possible internal bleeding)</li><li>A rigid, swollen or increasingly tender abdomen, or pain spreading to the left shoulder</li><li>Vomiting blood, blood in the urine or from the rectum after an injury</li><li>A seat-belt bruise across the belly after a crash</li></ul></div><div class="tri tri--amber"><h3>See a doctor within a day or two</h3><ul><li>Belly pain after a sports collision, handlebar injury or kick that is not settling over 1 to 2 hours</li><li>Any abdominal injury in someone taking blood thinners, in pregnancy, or in a young child</li></ul></div><div class="tri tri--blue"><h3>Book a routine appointment</h3><ul><li>Mild bruising of the belly wall without pain inside, after a minor knock, if checked by a clinician</li></ul></div></div></section>
<div class="prose">


<h2 id="first-aid-for-abdominal-injuries">First aid for abdominal injuries</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Call 911</strong> for any significant mechanism (car crash, fall from height, stab or gunshot) or worrying signs.</li><li><strong>Keep the person lying still</strong>, legs raised slightly if there are signs of shock and no spinal injury is suspected. Keep them warm.</li><li><strong>Do not give food or drink.</strong> They may need surgery.</li><li><strong>For a penetrating wound</strong>, press firmly around the wound with a clean cloth. <strong>Do not remove</strong> a knife or other object stuck in the body; pad around it to keep it still.</li><li><strong>If organs protrude</strong> through a wound, do not push them back. Cover them loosely with a clean, moist dressing or plastic wrap.</li><li><strong>Watch breathing and responsiveness</strong>, and start CPR if they stop breathing normally.</li></ol>
<h2 id="types-of-abdominal-trauma">Types of abdominal trauma</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Blunt trauma</th><th scope="col">Penetrating trauma</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">Causes</th><td>Car and motorcycle crashes, falls, sports, assaults, crush injuries</td><td>Stab wounds, gunshots, impalement</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Mechanism</th><td>Crushing, shearing, sudden deceleration</td><td>Direct cutting or tearing</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Organs most often hurt</th><td>Spleen, liver, kidneys, small bowel</td><td>Liver, small bowel, colon, blood vessels</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Challenge</th><td>Injuries hidden, signs may be delayed</td><td>Hard to judge depth from outside</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="organs-at-risk-and-their-signs">Organs at risk and their signs</h2>
<ul><li><strong><a href="/condition/spleen-injury/">Spleen</a>:</strong> the most commonly injured organ in blunt trauma. Left upper belly pain, often spreading to the left shoulder tip (Kehr's sign), and signs of blood loss. Risk is higher with an enlarged spleen, such as during mono.</li><li><strong><a href="/condition/liver-injury/">Liver</a>:</strong> right upper belly pain, sometimes right shoulder pain, and bleeding.</li><li><strong><a href="/condition/kidney-injury/">Kidneys</a>:</strong> flank pain and blood in the urine after blows to the back or side.</li><li><strong>Bowel:</strong> a tear or <a href="/condition/bowel-perforation/">perforation</a> leaks contents into the abdomen, causing <a href="/condition/peritonitis/">peritonitis</a> with fever and worsening pain over hours to days. Seat-belt injuries are a classic cause.</li><li><strong><a href="/condition/pancreatic-injury/">Pancreas</a>:</strong> upper belly or back pain, often after a handlebar injury in children; signs can be delayed.</li><li><strong>Diaphragm:</strong> a tear lets abdominal organs move into the chest, causing breathing difficulty.</li><li><strong>Major blood vessels:</strong> rapid, life-threatening bleeding.</li></ul>
<h3 id="the-seat-belt-sign">The seat-belt sign</h3>
<p>A bruise across the lower belly in the shape of a lap belt after a crash means a high-energy force was absorbed by the abdomen. It is linked with bowel injuries and fractures of the lower spine, and always needs a CT scan.</p>
<h2 id="symptoms-of-internal-injury">Symptoms of internal injury</h2>
<ul><li>abdominal pain that gets worse rather than better</li><li>tenderness, guarding (tensing when touched) or a rigid belly</li><li>swelling or distension</li><li>bruising on the belly, flanks or around the belly button</li><li>nausea and vomiting, especially with blood</li><li>blood in the urine</li><li>signs of shock: dizziness, fainting, fast heart rate, low blood pressure, pale clammy skin, confusion, fast breathing</li></ul>
<p>Children, older adults, intoxicated people and those with head or spinal injuries may not show clear signs. Bleeding from the spleen can also be delayed by hours or days.</p>
<h2 id="how-abdominal-trauma-is-diagnosed">How abdominal trauma is diagnosed</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Primary survey</strong> in the ER: airway, breathing, circulation and bleeding control.</li><li><strong>FAST scan (Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma):</strong> a bedside ultrasound that looks for free fluid (blood) in the abdomen in minutes.</li><li><strong>CT scan with contrast:</strong> the main test for stable patients; shows organ tears, bleeding and free air from bowel injury.</li><li><strong>Blood tests:</strong> hemoglobin, blood type and crossmatch, liver and pancreatic enzymes, and pregnancy tests.</li><li><strong>Urine test</strong> for blood.</li><li><strong>Repeated examination</strong> over hours, since some injuries become apparent later.</li></ul>
<h2 id="treatment">Treatment</h2>
<h3 id="unstable-patients">Unstable patients</h3>
<p>People in shock with signs of abdominal bleeding go straight to the operating room for <strong>exploratory laparotomy</strong>, often with <strong>damage control surgery</strong>: stopping bleeding and contamination quickly, then completing repairs once stable. Blood transfusions are given, and <a href="/condition/hemorrhagic-shock/">hemorrhagic shock</a> is treated aggressively.</p>
<h3 id="stable-patients">Stable patients</h3>
<p>Many blunt injuries to the spleen, liver and kidneys are now managed <strong>without surgery</strong>:</p>
<ul><li>close monitoring in hospital with bed rest and repeated blood counts</li><li><strong>angioembolization</strong>: a catheter passed through an artery blocks bleeding vessels</li><li>surgery if bleeding continues or a bowel injury is found</li></ul>
<p>Stab wounds may be explored locally, observed, or operated on depending on depth and findings. Most gunshot wounds to the abdomen need surgery.</p>
<h3 id="recovery">Recovery</h3>
<p>After a spleen or liver injury managed without surgery, contact sports and heavy lifting are usually avoided for several weeks to a few months. People who have the spleen removed need specific vaccines and education about infection risk.</p>
<h2 id="complications">Complications</h2>
<ul><li>delayed bleeding</li><li>infection, peritonitis and abscesses</li><li>pancreatic leaks or pseudocysts</li><li>bowel obstruction from adhesions later in life</li><li>abdominal compartment syndrome, where swelling raises pressure inside the abdomen</li></ul>
<h2 id="prevention">Prevention</h2>
<ul><li>Wear seat belts correctly: lap belt low across the hips, not the belly. Use proper car seats and boosters for children.</li><li>Wear protective gear in contact sports and on bikes and ATVs.</li><li>Pregnant people should place the lap belt below the bump and the shoulder belt between the breasts.</li></ul>
<section class="faqs"><h2 id="faq">Frequently asked questions</h2><details class="faq"><summary><h3>What are the signs of internal bleeding after a fall?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Warning signs include worsening belly pain, a swollen or rigid abdomen, bruising on the belly or flanks, pain in the shoulder tip, dizziness or fainting, a racing heart, pale clammy skin and confusion. These need emergency care. Signs can appear hours after the injury.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>Can you have an abdominal injury without pain at first?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. Adrenaline, other painful injuries, alcohol or a head injury can mask pain, and some injuries such as bowel tears or delayed spleen bleeding develop over hours. That is why anyone with a high-energy injury should be assessed, even if they feel fine.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>What is the most commonly injured organ in blunt abdominal trauma?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>The spleen is the most commonly injured organ in blunt abdominal trauma, followed by the liver. Both have a rich blood supply and can bleed heavily. Many such injuries are now treated without surgery under close monitoring.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>Do all abdominal injuries need surgery?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>No. Many blunt injuries to the spleen, liver and kidneys heal with observation or a catheter procedure to stop bleeding. Surgery is needed for ongoing bleeding with shock, bowel injuries, most gunshot wounds and some stab wounds.</p></div></details><details class="faq"><summary><h3>How long after a stomach injury should I watch for symptoms?</h3></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Watch closely for at least 24 to 48 hours after a significant blow. Delayed bleeding from the spleen can occur days later, rarely up to a couple of weeks. Seek care immediately for new or worsening pain, dizziness or vomiting.</p></div></details></section>
</div>
